{"id": 4424315, "name": "GILREATH v. GREENVILLE CO.", "name_abbreviation": "Gilreath v. Greenville Co.", "decision_date": "1905-02-02", "docket_number": "", "first_page": "389", "last_page": "391", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "70 S.C. 389"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "56 S. C., 411", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [9504049], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/56/0411-01"]}, {"cite": "40 S. C., 445", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [366360], "weight": 2, "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/40/0445-01"]}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 214, "char_count": 2876, "ocr_confidence": 0.559, "pagerank": {"raw": 1.036957840967046e-07, "percentile": 0.5496967197701488}, "sha256": "481071b1ae740391439de3cd0fa2466111da2b162b661800a8310bec011de179", "simhash": "1:18e53034c6a6ad93", "word_count": 483}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T17:27:51.434867+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["GILREATH v. GREENVILLE CO."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Gary.\nThis is a controversy without action, for the purpose of determining whether J. -D. Gilreath, as sheriff of Greenville County, is entitled to a fee of two dollars and twenty cents for services rendered in arresting a person under a warant, charging' him with assault and battery with intent to kill, issued by L. K. Clyde, a magistrate for the city of Greenville, S. C.\nHis Honor, the Circuit Judge, ruled that neither the general salary law nor the magistrates\u2019 and constables\u2019 acts of 1902, impose upon the sheriff of Greenville County the duty of serving process in magistrate courts without compensation. The sole question in the case is whether said ruling was erroneous.\nIn 1902 (page 1075), an act was passed, entitled \u201cAn act to fix the amount of the compensation to be paid to the county officers of the various counties of the State.\u201d Section 4 of that act provides., \u201cthat the sheriff of the various counties of this State, shall receive annual salaries in lieu of all costs and fees chargeable against the county, as follows: * * * Greenville, twenty-two' hundred dollars.\u201d Section 17 contains the usual clause repealing all acts or parts of acts inconsistent with the statute. The plain intention of the act is that the sheriff of Greenville County should not receive any compensation whatever except the salary of twenty-two hundred dollars, for the rendition of all services by him as sheriff theretofore chargeable against the county.\nIn section 3118 of the Code of Laws, prescribing fees for the sheriffs, is the following clause: \u201cFor the service or execution of papers issued by a magistrate, the sheriff or his deputy serving or executing the same, shall be allowed the same fees as are allowed to constables.\u201d This provision is inconsistent with the foregoing act of 1902, at least in so far as Greenville County is concerned, and is, therefore, inoperative in that county.\nIt is the judgment of this Court, that the judgment of the Circuit Court be reversed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Gary."}], "attorneys": ["Messrs. T. K. Borle and Shuman. & Muckenfuss, for appellant,", "Mr. Adam C. Welborn, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "GILREATH v. GREENVILLE CO.\nSaeary \u2014 Fees\u2014SheriEE.\u2014The salary act of 1902 gives the sheriff of Greenville County a salary for all services performed for county, and he cannot collect fees of county for serving papers issued in criminal case issued by magistrate.\nBefore Watts, J., Greenville, December, 1902.\nReversed.\nControversy without action of J. D. Gilreath against Greenville County. From Circuit order, defendant appeals.\nMessrs. T. K. Borle and Shuman. & Muckenfuss, for appellant,\ncite: 23 Stat., 1075; Code, 1902, 850;'23 Stat., 984-989; Code, 1902, 1024; 21 Stat., 375, 1085; 22. Stat., 478-22; 40 S. C., 445.\nMr. Adam C. Welborn, contra,\ncites: Gen. Stat., 2437; 18 Stat., 164; Rev. Stat., 2561; 22 Stat., 740; Code, 1902, 3118; 23 Stat., 989; 40 S. C., 445; 20 Stat., 462; 56 S. C., 411; 23 Stat., 1075.\nFebruary 2, 1905."}, "file_name": "0389-01", "first_page_order": 401, "last_page_order": 403}